cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, the audit pipeline, or the released
  diagnostics, please cite the underlying paper and this software
  release.
title: >-
  Direction is geometric, magnitude is not, and Bernoulli pressure
  drop alone deceives: a four-variant clinical-quantity audit of
  graph neural network surrogates for vascular blood flow
authors:
  - given-names: A. Daniel
    family-names: Cieslak
    email: cieslak.a.daniel@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  Patient-specific simulation of cardiovascular blood flow is
  computationally expensive, motivating graph neural network (GNN)
  surrogates that report near-CFD accuracy on aggregate metrics. We
  perform a four-variant feature-leakage audit across four flow
  domains (VMR aortas, Womersley pipe, Cosserat curved-tube sweep,
  synthetic U-bend CFD; ≈200 model–data combinations) and identify
  (i) an identifiability asymmetry — direction is geometric,
  magnitude requires boundary information — that recurs in every
  domain and (ii) a deceptive simplified-Bernoulli pressure-drop
  metric that prefers a magnitude-collapsed predictor whose per-frame
  correlation with ground truth is statistically zero. We propose a
  clinical-triplet reporting standard (pressure drop, wall shear
  stress, peak velocity localisation) and release the full audit
  pipeline.
license: MIT
